nd:rgba(52,211,153,.15);color:var(--emerald)}.story-card{background:linear-gradient(135deg,rgba(96,165,250,.06),rgba(167,139,250,.04));border:1px solid rgba(167,139,250,.12);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:24px;margin-bottom:20px;transition:all .2s;position:relative;overflow:hidden}.story-card:hover{border-color:rgba(167,139,250,.25);transform:translateY(-2px)}.story-label{display:inline-block;padding:4px 10px;border-radius:100px;font-size:.72em;font-weight:600;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.5px;background:rgba(167,139,250,.1);color:var(--purple);margin-bottom:8px}.story-title{font-size:1.2em;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:8px}.story-text{color:var(--text-sec);font-size:.88em;line-height:1.65}.story-moral{background:rgba(167,139,250,.06);border-left:3px solid var(--purple);border-radius:6px;padding:12px 16px;margin-top:12px;font-size:.84em;color:var(--text-sec)}.quote-box{background:rgba(52,211,153,.04);border:1px solid rgba(52,211,153,.15);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:24px;margin:20px 0}.quote{font-size:1.1em;font-weight:600;font-style:italic;color:var(--emerald);margin-bottom:6px}.attribution{font-size:.82em;color:var(--text-muted)}.section-divider{height:2px;background:linear-gradient(90deg,transparent,var(--purple),transparent);margin:36px 0}.section-label{display:inline-block;padding:4px 14px;border-radius:100px;font-size:.72em;font-weight:700;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.8px;margin-bottom:0}.part1{background:rgba(167,139,250,.12);color:var(--purple)}.part2{background:rgba(52,211,153,.12);color:var(--emerald)}.part3{background:rgba(251,146,60,.12);color:#fb923c}.part4{background:rgba(244,63,94,.12);color:#f43f5e}.q-card{background:var(--bg-card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:20px;margin-bottom:14px}.q-text{font-weight:600;margin-bottom:12px;font-size:.92em}.q-opts{display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:8px}.q-opt{padding:10px 14px;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid var(--border);cursor:pointer;font-size:.85em;transition:all .15s;color:var(--text-sec)}.q-opt:hover{border-color:var(--purple);background:rgba(167,139,250,.06)}.q-opt.correct{border-color:var(--emerald)!important;background:rgba(52,211,153,.1)!important;color:var(--emerald)!important}.q-opt.wrong{border-color:var(--red)!important;background:rgba(239,68,68,.1)!important;color:var(--red)!important}.q-opt.disabled{pointer-events:none;opacity:.7}.q-soln{display:none;margin-top:10px;padding:12px;background:rgba(167,139,250,.06);border-radius:8px;font-size:.82em;color:var(--text-sec);line-height:1.6}.q-soln.show{display:block}.nav-links a:hover{color:var(--text)}</style></head><body><nav class="nav"><div class="nav-inner"><span class="brand-text">vlymbooq</span><div class="nav-links"><a href="math.html">Math Map</a><a href="physics.html">Physics Map</a><a href="chemistry.html">Chemistry</a></div></div></nav><div class="container"><div class="lesson-body"><h2>Chemical Bonding — Ionic, Covalent, Metallic, VSEPR, MOT &amp; Hybridization</h2><p>Deep dive into Chemical Bonding — ionic bonding: electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions, lattice energy (U = −(N_A M z⁺ z⁻ e²)/(4π ε₀ r₀)(1 − 1/n) from the Born-Landé equation), the Born-Haber cycle (ΔH_f = ΔH_sub + IE + ½BDE + EA + U). Covalent bonding: Lewis electron-dot structures, the octet rule and its exceptions (expanded octets, odd-electron molecules, electron-deficient compounds), sigma bonds (head-on overlap along the internuclear axis) and pi bonds (sideways overlap above and below the axis), bond order (BO = ½(# bonding − # antibonding)), bond length and bond energy trends. Metallic bonding: the electron sea model (delocalized valence electrons), band theory (valence band, conduction band, band gap), conductors (overlapping or partially filled bands), semiconductors (small band gap), insulators (large band gap), doping (n-type and p-type semiconductors). VSEPR theory (Valence Shell Electron Pair Repulsion): electron domains (bonding pairs and lone pairs) repel to maximize separation, giving molecular geometries — linear 2, trigonal planar 3, tetrahedral 4, trigonal bipyramidal 5, octahedral 6; lone pairs occupy more space reducing bond angles. Molecular Orbital Theory: linear combination of atomic orbitals (LCAO) produces bonding MOs (lower energy, constructive interference) and antibonding MOs (higher energy, destructive interference, labeled with *), orbital mixing for heteronuclear diatomics, paramagnetism (O₂ has two unpaired electrons in π* orbitals), MO diagrams for homonuclear diatomic molecules (H₂, He₂, Li₂, Be₂, B₂, C₂, N₂, O₂, F₂, Ne₂). Hybridization: mixing of atomic orbitals to form equivalent hybrid orbitals — sp (linear, 180°, two hybrid orbitals), sp² (trigonal planar, 120°, three hybrids), sp³ (tetrahedral, 109.5°, four hybrids), dsp³ (trigonal bipyramidal, 90° and 120°, five hybrids), d²sp³ (octahedral, 90°, six hybrids).</p><div class="quote-box"><div class="quote">"A chemical bond is the force that holds atoms together in a compound."</div><div class="attribution">— Linus Pauling</div></div><div class="section-label part1">Part 1</div><h2>🔗 Ionic and Covalent Bonds</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Bonding Types</div><div class="story-title">Ionic Bonding, Lattice Energy, the Born-Haber Cycle, and Covalent Bonding Basics</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Ionic bonding</strong> occurs when a metal transfers one or more electrons to a nonmetal, forming cations and anions held together by electrostatic forces. The <strong>lattice energy</strong> U is the energy released when gaseous ions form one mole of an ionic solid. The Born-Landé equation: U = −(N_A M z⁺ z⁻ e²)/(4π ε₀ r₀)(1 − 1/n) where M is the Madelung constant (depends on crystal structure — 1.7476 for NaCl, 1.7627 for CsCl), z⁺ and z⁻ are ion charges, r₀ is the equilibrium interionic distance, and n is the Born exponent (≈ 9 for alkali halides). The <strong>Born-Haber cycle</strong> applies Hess's law to the formation of an ionic solid: ΔH_f° = ΔH_sub (metal sublimation) + IE (ionization energy) + ½ BDE (bond dissociation of the diatomic nonmetal) + EA (electron affinity) + U (lattice energy). <strong>Covalent bonding</strong> involves the sharing of electron pairs between atoms. The <strong>Lewis theory</strong> represents valence electrons as dots; atoms tend to achieve a noble-gas electron configuration (octet rule). Exceptions: molecules with odd numbers of electrons (NO, NO₂), electron-deficient molecules (BF₃, where B has only 6 electrons), and expanded octets (SF₆, PCl₅, where the central atom uses d orbitals). <strong>Sigma bonds (σ)</strong> are formed by end-on, head-to-head overlap along the internuclear axis — they are strong and cylindrically symmetric. <strong>Pi bonds (π)</strong> involve side-to-side overlap of p orbitals above and below the internuclear axis — they are weaker and prevent free rotation (as in double and triple bonds). A single bond is one σ bond; a double bond is σ + π; a triple bond is σ + 2π. <strong>Bond order</strong> = (number of bonding electrons − number of antibonding electrons)/2. Higher bond order means shorter bond length, higher bond energy, and greater bond stiffness.</div><div class="story-moral">Ionic bonding arises from electron transfer and electrostatic attraction quantified by lattice energy, while covalent bonding involves electron sharing with sigma and pi bonds determining bond strength and geometry.</div></div><div class="section-label part2">Part 2</div><h2>🔩 Metallic Bonding and Band Theory</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Metals</div><div class="story-title">Electron Sea Model, Band Theory, Semiconductors, and Conductivity</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>electron sea model</strong> describes metallic bonding as a lattice of positive metal ions immersed in a 'sea' of delocalized valence electrons that are free to move throughout the crystal. This explains metallic properties: high electrical and thermal conductivity (mobile electrons), malleability and ductility (layers of ions can slide without breaking bonds because electron sea redistributes), and metallic luster (electrons absorb and re-emit photons). <strong>Band theory</strong> refines this picture using molecular orbital theory extended to a macroscopic crystal. The atomic orbitals of N atoms combine to form N molecular orbitals that are so closely spaced they create continuous <strong>bands</strong> of energy levels. The <strong>valence band</strong> is the highest occupied band; the <strong>conduction band</strong> is the lowest unoccupied band; the <strong>band gap</strong> E_g is the energy difference between them. <strong>Conductors</strong> (metals) have either overlapping valence and conduction bands or a partially filled valence band — electrons move freely under an electric field. <strong>Insulators</strong> have a large band gap (E_g > 3 eV) — electrons cannot jump to the conduction band. <strong>Semiconductors</strong> (Si, Ge) have a small band gap (≈ 0.7−1.1 eV) — at room temperature, some electrons are thermally excited to the conduction band. Doping introduces impurities: <strong>n-type doping</strong> (Group V element like P or As) adds extra electrons to the conduction band; <strong>p-type doping</strong> (Group III element like B or Al) creates 'holes' (electron vacancies) in the valence band that conduct positive charge. The p-n junction is the basis of diodes, transistors, and solar cells. The conductivity σ = n e μ, where n is carrier concentration and μ is mobility. Temperature dependence: metals have decreasing conductivity with T (phonon scattering); semiconductors have increasing conductivity with T (more carriers excited).</div><div class="story-moral">Band theory explains the difference between conductors, semiconductors, and insulators by the size of the band gap and the filling of the valence and conduction bands.</div></div><div class="section-label part3">Part 3</div><h2>📐 VSEPR Theory and Molecular Geometry</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">Geometry</div><div class="story-title">Electron Domains, Molecular Shapes, and Lone-Pair Effects on Bond Angles</div><div class="story-text">The <strong>VSEPR (Valence Shell Electron Pair Repulsion) theory</strong>, developed by Gillespie and Nyholm, states that electron groups (bonding pairs and lone pairs) around a central atom repel each other and arrange to minimize repulsion — maximizing angular separation. <strong>Electron domain geometry</strong> describes the arrangement of all electron groups; <strong>molecular geometry</strong> describes the arrangement of atoms only. Total domains 2: linear (180°) — e.g., BeCl₂, CO₂. Total domains 3: trigonal planar (120°) — e.g., BF₃, SO₃; with 1 lone pair → bent (V-shaped) — e.g., SO₂, O₃ (≈ 119°). Total domains 4: tetrahedral (109.5°) — e.g., CH₄, NH₄⁺; with 1 lone pair → trigonal pyramidal — e.g., NH₃ (≈ 107°); with 2 lone pairs → bent — e.g., H₂O (≈ 104.5°). Lone pairs occupy more angular space than bonding pairs because they are held closer to the nucleus (the lone-pair electron cloud is more diffuse). The repulsion order: lone-pair/lone-pair > lone-pair/bonding-pair > bonding-pair/bonding-pair. Total domains 5: trigonal bipyramidal (90° and 120°) — e.g., PCl₅; lone pairs occupy equatorial positions preferentially (more space) giving see-saw (SF₄), T-shaped (ClF₃), or linear (XeF₂) geometries. Total domains 6: octahedral (90°) — e.g., SF₆; with 1 lone pair → square pyramidal (BrF₅); with 2 lone pairs → square planar (XeF₄). VSEPR also predicts deviations from ideal angles due to ligand electronegativity (more electronegative ligands reduce bond angles by pulling electron density away) and multiple bonds (treated as single electron domains but exert slightly greater repulsion).</div><div class="story-moral">VSEPR theory uses simple electrostatic repulsion between electron domains to predict molecular geometries remarkably well, with lone pairs exerting greater repulsive force than bonding pairs.</div></div><div class="section-label part4">Part 4</div><h2>🔬 Molecular Orbital Theory and Hybridization</h2><div class="story-card"><div class="story-label">MO &amp; Hybridization</div><div class="story-title">LCAO, Bonding/Antibonding MOs, Paramagnetism, and Hybrid Orbital Schemes</div><div class="story-text"><strong>Molecular Orbital (MO) theory</strong> treats electrons as delocalized over the entire molecule. Atomic orbitals combine via <strong>linear combination of atomic orbitals (LCAO)</strong> to form molecular orbitals: a bonding MO (constructive interference, lower energy, electrons concentrated between nuclei) and an antibonding MO* (destructive interference, higher energy, a node between nuclei). The number of MOs equals the number of AOs combined. For homonuclear diatomic molecules Li₂ through N₂, the orbital ordering is: σ₁s, σ*₁s, σ₂s, σ*₂s, π₂p_x = π₂p_y, σ₂p_z, π*₂p_x = π*₂p_y, σ*₂p_z. For O₂, F₂, Ne₂, the σ₂p_z drops below the π₂p set. O₂ is notable for having two unpaired electrons in π*₂p orbitals (paramagnetic — attracted to a magnetic field), confirmed experimentally. <strong>Bond order</strong> = (e⁻ in bonding − e⁻ in antibonding)/2. BO = 1 for H₂, 0 for He₂, 1 for Li₂, 0 for Be₂, 1 for B₂, 2 for C₂, 3 for N₂, 2 for O₂, 1 for F₂, 0 for Ne₂. <strong>Hybridization</strong> mixes nonequivalent atomic orbitals of a central atom to create equivalent hybrid orbitals with the correct geometry for bonding. <strong>sp hybridization</strong> (one s + one p → two sp hybrids, 180° linear) — BeCl₂, C₂H₂. <strong>sp² hybridization</strong> (s + 2p → three sp² hybrids, 120° trigonal planar) — BF₃, C₂H₄. <strong>sp³ hybridization</strong> (s + 3p → four sp³ hybrids, 109.5° tetrahedral) — CH₄, NH₃, H₂O. <strong>dsp³ hybridization</strong> (d + s + 3p → five dsp³ hybrids, trigonal bipyramidal) — PCl₅, PF₅. <strong>d²sp³ hybridization</strong> (2d + s + 3p → six d²sp³ hybrids, octahedral) — SF₆, [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻. In each case, the number of hybrid orbitals equals the number of atomic orbitals mixed. Unhybridized p or d orbitals remain for π bonding (as in ethene, where C uses sp² hybrids for σ bonds and the remaining p orbital forms the π bond).</div><div class="story-moral">MO theory provides a delocalized description of bonding that correctly predicts paramagnetism (O₂), while hybridization bridges the gap between atomic orbitals and the observed geometry of covalent bonds.</div></div><div class="section-divider"></div><h2 style="margin-top:0">🎯 Practice</h2><div id="practice-qs"></div><div style="margin-top:16px"><button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="resetPractice()">🔄 Reset All</button><a href="chemistry.html" class="btn btn-success">← Back to Chemistry</a></div></div></div><script>
var practiceQs=[{id:46013,text:"🔗 According to the Born-Haber cycle, which of the following contributes a positive ΔH to the formation of an ionic solid?",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Lattice energy",c:false},{l:"B",t:"Electron affinity",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Ionization energy",c:true},{l:"D",t:"Formation of the ionic solid from gaseous ions",c:false}],sol:"The correct answer is <strong>C</strong>: Ionization energy (removing an electron from a metal atom) is always endothermic (positive). Lattice energy and electron affinity are exothermic (negative)."},{id:46014,text:"🔗 A double bond consists of:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"One σ bond and one π bond",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Two σ bonds",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Two π bonds",c:false},{l:"D",t:"One σ bond and two π bonds",c:false}],sol:"The correct answer is <strong>A</strong>: A double bond always comprises one sigma bond (head-on overlap) and one pi bond (sideways overlap). A triple bond is one σ + two π."},{id:46015,text:"🔗 The Madelung constant for the NaCl structure is approximately:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"1.7476",c:true},{l:"B",t:"1.7627",c:false},{l:"C",t:"2.5194",c:false},{l:"D",t:"1.0000",c:false}],sol:"The correct answer is <strong>A</strong>: The Madelung constant for the NaCl (rock salt) structure is 1.7476. For CsCl it is 1.7627."},{id:46016,text:"🔩 In band theory, the difference between a conductor and an insulator is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"The size of the band gap between valence and conduction bands",c:true},{l:"B",t:"The number of protons in the nucleus",c:false},{l:"C",t:"The density of the material",c:false},{l:"D",t:"The atomic radius of the atoms",c:false}],sol:"The correct answer is <strong>A</strong>: Conductors have overlapping bands or a partially filled valence band (no gap), insulators have a large band gap (E_g > 3 eV), and semiconductors have a small gap."},{id:46017,text:"🔩 Which type of doping introduces extra electrons into the conduction band of a semiconductor?",opts:[{l:"A",t:"n-type",c:true},{l:"B",t:"p-type",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Intrinsic",c:false},{l:"D",t:"pn-junction",c:false}],sol:"The correct answer is <strong>A</strong>: n-type doping uses Group V elements (P, As) that donate an extra electron, increasing conduction band electron concentration."},{id:46018,text:"🔩 The conductivity of a semiconductor increases with temperature because:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"More electrons are thermally excited to the conduction band",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Phonon scattering decreases",c:false},{l:"C",t:"The band gap shrinks to zero",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Electron mobility increases indefinitely",c:false}],sol:"The correct answer is <strong>A</strong>: Higher temperature excites more electrons across the band gap, increasing carrier concentration n (σ = n e μ), which dominates over decreased mobility from phonon scattering."},{id:46019,text:"📐 According to VSEPR theory, what is the molecular geometry of NH₃?",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Trigonal pyramidal",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Trigonal planar",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Tetrahedral",c:false},{l:"D",t:"Bent",c:false}],sol:"The correct answer is <strong>A</strong>: NH₃ has 4 electron domains (3 bonding pairs + 1 lone pair), giving a tetrahedral electron domain geometry but a trigonal pyramidal molecular geometry."},{id:46020,text:"📐 The bond angle in H₂O is approximately 104.5°, smaller than the ideal tetrahedral angle of 109.5° because:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"Lone pairs exert greater repulsion than bonding pairs",c:true},{l:"B",t:"Oxygen is very electronegative",c:false},{l:"C",t:"Hydrogen atoms are small",c:false},{l:"D",t:"The molecule is linear",c:false}],sol:"The correct answer is <strong>A</strong>: The two lone pairs on oxygen exert stronger repulsion than bonding pairs, compressing the H-O-H bond angle (repulsion order: lp/lp > lp/bp > bp/bp)."},{id:46021,text:"📐 Which of the following molecules has a linear geometry according to VSEPR?",opts:[{l:"A",t:"CO₂",c:true},{l:"B",t:"SO₂",c:false},{l:"C",t:"H₂O",c:false},{l:"D",t:"NH₃",c:false}],sol:"The correct answer is <strong>A</strong>: CO₂ has 2 double bonds and no lone pairs on carbon (2 electron domains), giving a linear geometry with 180° bond angle."},{id:46022,text:"🔬 Which molecule is paramagnetic according to MO theory?",opts:[{l:"A",t:"O₂",c:true},{l:"B",t:"N₂",c:false},{l:"C",t:"F₂",c:false},{l:"D",t:"CO",c:false}],sol:"The correct answer is <strong>A</strong>: O₂ has two unpaired electrons in its π*₂p antibonding orbitals (bond order 2, but paramagnetic). N₂ and F₂ have all electrons paired (diamagnetic)."},{id:46023,text:"🔬 The hybridization of the central carbon atom in ethene (C₂H₄) is:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"sp²",c:true},{l:"B",t:"sp",c:false},{l:"C",t:"sp³",c:false},{l:"D",t:"dsp³",c:false}],sol:"The correct answer is <strong>A</strong>: Each carbon in C₂H₄ is sp² hybridized (trigonal planar, 120°), using three sp² hybrids for σ bonds and the remaining p orbital for the π bond."},{id:46024,text:"🔬 For the molecule SF₆, the hybridization of sulfur and the molecular geometry are:",opts:[{l:"A",t:"d²sp³, octahedral",c:true},{l:"B",t:"sp³, tetrahedral",c:false},{l:"C",t:"dsp³, trigonal bipyramidal",c:false},{l:"D",t:"sp², trigonal planar",c:false}],sol:"The correct answer is <strong>A</strong>: SF₆ requires six equivalent S-F bonds, which requires d²sp³ hybridization of sulfur, giving an octahedral geometry with 90° bond angles."}];var answered={},correctCount=0;function renderPractice(){var c=document.getElementById('practice-qs'),h='';for(var i=0;i<practiceQs.length;i++){var q=practiceQs[i],o='';for(var j=0;j<q.opts.length;j++){o+='<div class="q-opt" onclick="selectOpt('+q.id+','+j+')">'+q.opts[j].l+'. '+q.opts[j].t+'</div>';}h+='<div class="q-card" id="q-'+q.id+'"><div class="q-text">'+(i+1)+'. '+q.text+'</div><div class="q-opts">'+o+'</div><div class="q-soln" id="soln-'+q.id+'"><strong>'+getCorrectLabel(q)+'</strong><br>'+q.sol+'</div></div>';}c.innerHTML=h;}function getCorrectLabel(q){for(var i=0;i<q.opts.length;i++){if(q.opts[i].c)return q.opts[i].l+'. '+q.opts[i].t;}return '';}function selectOpt(qId,idx){if(answered[qId])return;answered[qId]=true;var q=practiceQs.filter(function(x){return x.id===qId})[0];var opts=document.querySelectorAll('#q-'+qId+' .q-opt'),isCorrect=q.opts[idx].c;for(var i=0;i<opts.length;i++)opts[i].classList.add('disabled');if(isCorrect){opts[idx].classList.add('correct');correctCount++;}else{opts[idx].classList.add('wrong');for(var i=0;i<q.opts.length;i++){if(q.opts[i].c)opts[i].classList.add('correct');}}document.getElementById('soln-'+qId).classList.add('show');}function resetPractice(){if(!confirm('Reset?'))return;answered={};correctCount=0;document.querySelectorAll('.q-opt').forEach(function(e){e.className='q-opt'});document.querySelectorAll('.q-soln').forEach(function(e){e.classList.remove('show')});renderPractice();}renderPractice();</script></body></html>